From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 00:42:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/venus: report protected memory off

Venus implements no protected memory of its own. It forwards whatever the host
driver returns from vkGetPhysicalDeviceFeatures2, which puts the guest in the
position of advertising a capability neither layer can honour.

Android takes the advertisement at face value. SkiaVkRenderEngine's dual-filter
Kawase blur preallocates protected buffers whenever the device reports
protectedMemory, and on a host whose driver reports it that preallocation fails
and SurfaceFlinger restarts, over and over, with no other symptom pointing at
the feature bit.

Clear it in the same fixup block that already turns off
accelerationStructureHostCommands and the unsupported
ExtendedDynamicState3Features, for the same reason: the protocol cannot carry
it.

Note on where this bites. A host running V3DV reports protectedMemory = false
itself (src/broadcom/vulkan/v3dv_device.c), so on a Raspberry Pi this change is
defensive rather than load-bearing; the restart loop was measured on an
Intel-hosted run, where the host driver does report it.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
--- a/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_physical_device.c
+++ b/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_physical_device.c
@@ -439,6 +439,15 @@
       feats->extendedDynamicState3RepresentativeFragmentTestEnable = false;
       feats->extendedDynamicState3ShadingRateImageEnable = false;
    }
+
+   /* Venus has no protected memory support of its own; it reports whatever the
+    * host driver reports. Android's SkiaVkRenderEngine takes that at face
+    * value: the dual-filter Kawase blur preallocates protected buffers when
+    * the device advertises protectedMemory, and on a host whose driver does
+    * advertise it that preallocation fails and takes SurfaceFlinger down in a
+    * restart loop. Report it off.
+    */
+   feats->protectedMemory = false;
 }
 
 static void
